course: dictionary
lesson: 13258
title: Glimpse: a quick look
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A glimpse is a very quick look at something, so you see it only for a moment or only part of it. «She caught a glimpse of the cat's tail disappearing under the fence.» She did not see the whole cat, just a quick part of it.
explain: As a verb, glimpse means to see something for a moment, often by chance. «Sam glimpsed a figure in the mist.» Sam did not study the figure. He saw it briefly, and perhaps by accident.
explain: A glimpse can also be a short experience that shows you what something is like, as in «a glimpse of the future». Read the words around glimpse to tell which meaning fits, then swap in your meaning to check it still makes sense.
fact: Glimpse can be a noun (a glimpse) or a verb (to glimpse, glimpsed, glimpsing).
fact: «A glimpse» means a very quick look, so you see only a moment or part of something.
fact: «Glimpsed» means saw something for a moment, often by chance.
fact: «A glimpse of the future» means a short experience that shows what something is like.
fact: In history, a find can give «a glimpse into» how people lived long ago.
exercise: «She caught a glimpse of the owl as it flew past the window.» What does glimpse mean here? | A cold drink on a hot day | A loud shout across the field | A long, careful study of a map | *A very quick look at something
exercise: «He glimpsed a figure in the mist.» What does glimpsed mean? | *Saw for a moment, often by chance | Forgot completely | Heard a sound from far away | Painted carefully
exercise: A museum find gives us a glimpse into how people lived long ago. Which phrase means the same as glimpse here? | *A short look that shows what it was like | A huge crowd of visitors | A heavy box of old tools | A long holiday by the sea
test: «Through the gap in the hedge, Tom glimpsed a rabbit.» In this sentence, what kind of word is glimpsed? | An adjective | A full stop | A noun | *A verb
test: «The film gave us a glimpse of the future.» What does a glimpse mean here? | *A short experience that shows what something is like | A long, slow walk to the door | A ticket to the next showing | A very loud noise from the cinema
exam: «Maya had only a glimpse of the moon through the clouds.» What does only a glimpse suggest? | She painted the moon in the sky | *She saw a little of it, for a moment | She saw the whole moon clearly for hours | She could not see the moon at all
